

**28. Scales of Notation**

**Representation of Numbers**

Any positive real number \(x\) can be uniquely represented in the scale of some integer _b_>1 as

\[x = (A_{m} \cdot \cdot \cdot A_{1}A_{0} \cdot a_{-1}a_{-2} \cdot \cdot \cdot)_{(0)},\]

where every \(A_{t}\) and _a__-__j_ is one of the integers 0, 1, . ., _b_-1, not all \(A_{t}\), _a__-__j_ are zero, and _A__n_>0 if \(x\) >=1. There is a one-to-one correspondence between the number and the sequence

\[x = A_{n}b^{m} + \cdot \cdot \cdot +A_{1}b + A_{0} + \frac{*}{12}a_{-7}b^{-1}\]

where the infinite series converges. The integer \(b\) is called the base or radix of the scale.

The sequence for \(x\) in the scale of \(b\) may terminate, i.e., _a__-_n_-1=_a__-_n_-2= \(\cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot = 0\) for some \(n\) >=1 so that

\[x = (A_{m} \cdot \cdot A_{1}A_{0} \cdot a_{-1}a_{-2} \cdot \cdot \cdot a_{-3})_{(0)};\]

then \(x\) is said to be a finite _b_-adic number.

A sequence which does not terminate may have the property that the infinite sequence _a__-_1, _a__-_2, . . becomes periodic from a certain digit _a__-_n_(_n_ >=1 on; according as \(n\) = 1 or \(n\) >1 the sequence is then said to be pure or mixed recurring.

A sequence which neither terminates nor recurs represents an irrational number.

